  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Hessel's Hairstreak is a small, delicate butterfly belonging to the Gossamer-winged Butterfly family, characterized by its intricate wing patterns and subtle coloration. This rare and endangered species is found exclusively in the northeastern United States, particularly in New England, where it depends on Atlantic white cedar trees for its survival.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Wing coloration",
      "description": "Grayish-green upperside with faint orange spots near the hindwing tail; iridescent blue on the underside of the hindwing",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Tail-like projections",
      "description": "Small, hair-like tails on the hindwings that give the hairstreak family its common name",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Size",
      "description": "Very tiny butterfly with a wingspan of only 0.9 to 1.1 inches, making it difficult to spot",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Hessel's Hairstreak is found in coastal Atlantic white cedar swamps and pocosins in New England, where its host plant thrives in moist, acidic environments.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "This butterfly is so rare and localized that it was thought to be extinct until its rediscovery in the 1990s, and today it remains one of the most endangered butterflies in North America.",
